15:15:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Traditionally—and even in most new-build houses—UK upper floors are framed with timber joists and covered with floorboards or chipboard. Concrete is rarely used except in larger apartment blocks. The choice comes down to a mix of history, economics, structural efficiency, and practicality. ### 1. Historical tradition and material availability The UK has a long building heritage of timber framing. After the Great Fire of London (1666) and later building acts, outer walls shifted to brick or stone, but internal floors remained timber. Timber was abundant, workable with simple tools, and required no heavy lifting gear. That tradition has persisted simply because it works and the industry is comfortable with it. ### 2. Weight and structural economy Concrete is heavy. A typical 150 mm reinforced concrete slab weighs around 3.6 kN/m², whereas a timber joist floor with chipboard is less than 1 kN/m². This extra weight would mean: - Stronger (more expensive) foundations - Thicker load‑bearing walls or more steel - Greater seismic mass (relevant in some design, though UK seismicity is low) In a two‑storey house, the savings in materials and ground‑works are significant when you keep upper floors light. ### 3. Speed, cost, and ease of construction Timber joists are quick to install. A small team can manually lift and fix them without a crane. Services (plumbing, wiring, heating ducts) are easily routed through the open web or between solid joists, and adjustments on site are straightforward. Concrete would need: - Shuttering (formwork) or precast planks - Curing time if cast in situ - Pre‑planned service penetrations or surface mounted conduits All of this adds time and cost, which is unjustified in a typical two‑storey house. ### 4. Thermal performance Timber has natural insulating properties. While UK Building Regulations now require insulation between and under floors (and often a warm roof), concrete is a thermal bridge that draws heat out. Without substantial additional insulation, a concrete upper floor would make upstairs rooms colder and increase condensation risk at the perimeter. Timber floors, combined with plasterboard ceilings and mineral wool between joists, hit thermal and acoustic targets more economically. ### 5. Sound insulation – not a primary concern for houses Concrete is undeniably better at reducing airborne sound, which is why flats and apartment buildings often use concrete or composite separating floors to meet stringent acoustic requirements (Part E of the Building Regulations). In a single‑family house, however, sound transfer between your own living room and bedroom is less critical. Timber floors with carpet, underlay, and a plasterboard ceiling offer perfectly adequate sound control for a detached or semi‑detached house. ### 6. Fire resistance – modern timber solutions meet the codes Timber has a predictable charring rate, and joists can be oversized or combined with fire‑resistant plasterboard to achieve the required 30‑minute (or sometimes 60‑minute) fire resistance. Concrete, meanwhile, would need much thicker sections and still require fire protection to reinforcement. Building Regulations (Approved Document B) accept timber‑based floor assemblies when properly detailed. ### 7. Adaptability over the life of the house Homeowners routinely lift floorboards to add sockets, install network cables, or repair plumbing. A timber floor makes renovations trivial compared to chasing into concrete or adding surface trunking. This flexibility is a quiet but important reason why both homeowners and builders prefer timber. ### The concrete exception: flats and beam‑and‑block When you move to multi‑occupancy buildings (flats, apartments), the calculations change. Fire compartmentation and airborne/noise separation between different households become critical, and the extra dead weight is spread across more supporting structure. Here, precast concrete planks (beam‑and‑block) or in‑situ concrete slabs are common. But for a typical two‑storey house, timber remains the lightest, cheapest, and most buildable solution.
